THIRD-PARTY ATTRIBUTIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

hausdorff replaces the external geospatial stack with in-house implementations.
Several of those are ports rather than independent work, and the ported code
keeps its upstream copyright. Each entry below names what was taken, where it
now lives, and the licence it arrived under.

The grid-shift data files are the exception to "code": they are redistributed
verbatim as published, and are covered by their issuing agency's terms rather
than by this repository's licence.


1. georust `geo` / `geo-types`
   Licensed under MIT OR Apache-2.0.
   Copyright (c) The GeoRust Developers.
   https://github.com/georust/geo

   The planar f64 geometry types and algorithms in `hausdorff-planar`
   (`space::r2`) are a port of `geo`/`geo-types`.


2. S2 Geometry, Go implementation (`github.com/golang/geo/s2`)
   Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
   Copyright Google Inc.
   https://github.com/golang/geo

   The unit-sphere geometry and cell hierarchy in `hausdorff-sphere`
   (`space::s2`) are a port of the Go S2 reference implementation: the cell-id
   codec, the cube-to-sphere quadratic projection, the hierarchy and neighbor
   operations, Hilbert-order traversal, the robust orientation predicates and
   measures, caps, loops, polylines, and region coverings. The `s1` angle
   substrate in `hausdorff-base` mirrors `s1.Angle`.


3. H3 — Hexagonal hierarchical geospatial indexing system
   Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
   Copyright Uber Technologies, Inc.
   https://github.com/uber/h3

   The H3 implementation in `hausdorff-index` (`index::h3`) is ported from the
   H3 C reference implementation. The constant tables — base cells, faces, and
   the FaceIJK base-cell lookup — are the standard H3 data, lifted verbatim.

   The `h3o` Rust crate (https://github.com/HydroniumLabs/h3o) was used as a
   differential-test oracle while the port was developed. It is not a
   dependency of this workspace; the H3 suite now checks against golden
   constants frozen from the then-h3o-matching output.


4. GeographicLib / `geographiclib-rs`
   Licensed under the MIT/X11 License.
   Copyright (c) Charles F. F. Karney.
   https://geographiclib.sourceforge.io/
   https://github.com/georust/geographiclib-rs

   The ellipsoidal geodesic kernel in `hausdorff-base` (`earth::karney`) is
   ported from `geographiclib-rs` 0.2.7, itself a port of Karney's
   GeographicLib. The algorithms are published in C. F. F. Karney,
   "Algorithms for geodesics", J. Geodesy 87, 43-55 (2013).


5. NTv2 grid-shift data files (redistributed data, not code)
   Embedded under `crates/hausdorff-earth/data/` and compiled into the library
   with `include_bytes!`. Each file is the official agency distribution,
   unmodified, and remains subject to the terms its issuing agency publishes it
   under — not to this repository's licence. Verify those terms before
   redistributing a build that embeds them.

   - `BETA2007.gsb` — DHDN <-> ETRS89.
     Bundesamt fuer Kartographie und Geodaesie (BKG), Germany.

   - `ntf_r93.gsb` — NTF <-> RGF93.
     Institut national de l'information geographique et forestiere (IGN),
     France.

   - `nzgd2kgrid0005.gsb` — NZGD49 <-> NZGD2000.
     Land Information New Zealand (LINZ).


6. Map projection formulas
   J. P. Snyder, "Map Projections — A Working Manual", USGS Professional Paper
   1395 (1987). A work of the United States Government and so not under
   copyright. The projection series in `hausdorff-kernels` (`projection`) and
   `hausdorff-earth` (`earth::projection`) implement these published formulas;
   no code was taken.

   The CRS interchange formats are likewise implemented from their published
   specifications rather than ported: PROJ-string parameter names, WKT2
   (ISO 19162:2019), PROJJSON, OGC/ISO Well-Known Binary and Text, GeoJSON
   (RFC 7946), and the ESRI Shapefile technical description.
